# client-billing-settings.steps-collection-vocabulary.must-fail.patch
#
# Targets: @AC-1 — the collection's error-flag name leaking back into the
# manager-bound catalog (the exact drift class the replay exists to catch).
#
# Mutates ONE test artefact — the step CATALOG, never production source:
#   packages/headless/src/modules/client-billing-settings/__tests__/client-billing-settings.steps.ts
# Renames exactly one flag in the AC-1 `Then` handler, whose phrasing line
# ("the five values I see are the ones actually saved against my own record")
# is what makes the hunk unique — several step bodies are textually identical:
#   world.expectMeta({ isAvailable: true, hasErrors: false })
#     -> world.expectMeta({ isAvailable: true, hasError:  false })
# Nothing else changes. `hasError` is the COLLECTION's (`useList`) meta key;
# `hasErrors` is the MANAGER's (`useMutate`). This catalog boots the manager
# alone (a single form — `client-billing-settings`), so `hasError` names a
# member the booted composable never publishes.
#
# Spec that MUST go RED (a SEPARATE seat verifies this — the author does not):
#   client-billing-settings.replay.int.test.ts
#   Scenario: @AC-1 My preference shows my actual saved values, addressed to my own record
#
# Expected RED — the world's "unknown member" failure mode (not a value
# mismatch), because `hasError` is absent from the manager's meta entirely:
#   node-world: unknown meta member(s) hasError — the booted composable publishes ...
# (the "..." is the manager's real meta key set, printed at the mismatch; the
# load-bearing fact is that `hasError` is NOT among them.) AC-1 is currently
# GREEN in the replay; only AC-1 flips RED, since its `Then` phrasing is unique.
#
# Seat nuance (agent-seat-separation.companion.md): the base rule routes a
# mutant to the DEVELOPER because a mutant normally mutates PRODUCTION source,
# and reverting/breaking a production line is a code mutation the prover must
# not author from a blind read of src. This mutant's target is a TEST artefact
# — the step catalog — which sits inside the PROVER's own write lane, so the
# PROVER authors it (no production source read to construct it) and a SEPARATE
# seat applies it blind, confirms the RED, and reverts. Author-of-mutation !=
# verifier-of-red holds either way.
#
# Apply / verify / revert (run by the VERIFYING seat, not the author):
#   git apply <this file>                                    # mutate
#   pnpm --filter @upmind-automation/headless exec vitest run \
#     src/modules/client-billing-settings/__tests__/client-billing-settings.replay.int.test.ts   # @AC-1 must go RED
#   git apply -R <this file>                                  # revert — back to green
#
--- a/packages/headless/src/modules/client-billing-settings/__tests__/client-billing-settings.steps.ts
+++ b/packages/headless/src/modules/client-billing-settings/__tests__/client-billing-settings.steps.ts
@@ -95,5 +95,5 @@
     Then(
       "the five values I see are the ones actually saved against my own record",
       world =>
-        settles(() => world.expectMeta({ isAvailable: true, hasErrors: false }))
+        settles(() => world.expectMeta({ isAvailable: true, hasError: false }))
     );
